# SteamSwitch Privacy Policy

SteamSwitch is a local desktop application. It has no accounts, no sign-up, and no
backend service operated by its author.

## What the app does with your data

SteamSwitch works entirely by moving files and registry values that Steam uses to
remember its last login, while Steam is closed. None of those files, nor any
information from them, is uploaded anywhere. Everything stays on your computer,
usually under `%AppData%\SteamSwitch` unless you choose another location in settings.

No passwords are ever handled: the app swaps Steam's own saved-login data; it never
sees or stores your Steam credentials.

## Network requests the app can make

All outbound requests exist only to display information to you, and all of them can be
disabled with Offline Mode (a transport-level block inside the app):

- `steamcommunity.com` — per-account profile name, avatar and VAC/limited status
  (`https://steamcommunity.com/profiles/{SteamId}?xml=1`). Can be disabled in
  Steam settings ("Collect profile images and VAC ban status").
- `api.steampowered.com` — Valve's public app-name list, used to show game names.
- Optional game-statistics providers listed in `GameStats.json` — used only if you
  explicitly enable a game's stats for an account; the data source is shown when you
  enable it.
- Discord Rich Presence talks only to your local Discord client, never the network.

## What the fork removed

The upstream project (TcNo Account Switcher) offered opt-in anonymous statistics,
crash-report upload, feedback submission and update checks against its own servers.
This fork removed all of that: there is no telemetry, no crash upload, no feedback
endpoint and no phone-home update check. Usage statistics and crash logs are still
written locally for your own use; they never leave your PC.

